        <h2>SolveCalc Calculator Guide</h2>

        <h3>What Is a CalcSolver?</h3>
        <div class="sc-ad sc-ad-section" id="sc-gam-whatis"></div>
        <p>A <strong>calcsolver</strong> is a free online calculator that solves math problems instantly in your browser - no app, no download, no account. SolveCalc is a full calcsolver: type or click an expression and it handles arithmetic, algebra, trigonometry, logarithms, powers, roots, fractions, and percentages straight away.</p>
        <p>People type it as <strong>calcsolver</strong> or <strong>calc solver</strong>, but they mean the same thing: a fast online math solver you can open and use in seconds. SolveCalc works on phones, school Chromebooks, and desktops, and every tool on the site is free.</p>

        <h4>What Makes SolveCalc a Good CalcSolver</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Instant, no sign-up</strong> - open the page and start calculating immediately.</li>
          <li><strong>Full scientific functions</strong> - sin, cos, tan, log, ln, square roots, exponents, factorials, and parentheses for order of operations.</li>
          <li><strong>20+ dedicated tools</strong> - beyond the main calc solver there are fraction, percentage, equation, geometry, statistics, and graphing calculators.</li>
          <li><strong>Works on school devices</strong> - built to run on Chromebooks and locked-down school networks where apps can't be installed.</li>
          <li><strong>Seven calculator themes</strong> - including a scientific device layout, so it looks and feels how you want.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Keyboard Shortcuts</h3>
        <p>You don't have to click anything. The calculator listens to your keyboard as soon as the page loads, which is much faster on a laptop or Chromebook. Every key below works right now:</p>
        <table><caption class="visually-hidden">Keyboard Shortcuts</caption>
          <tr><th>Key</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>0</kbd> - <kbd>9</kbd></td><td>Type digits</td></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>.</kbd></td><td>Decimal point</td></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>+</kbd> <kbd>-</kbd> <kbd>*</kbd> <kbd>/</kbd></td><td>Add, subtract, multiply, divide</td></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>=</kbd></td><td>Calculate the result</td></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>Backspace</kbd></td><td>Delete the last character</td></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Clear everything (AC)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>(</kbd> <kbd>)</kbd></td><td>Open and close brackets</td></tr>
          <tr><td><kbd>%</kbd></td><td>Percent</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p>Typing in the scratch notes below the calculator won't trigger these - the keyboard only drives the calculator when the notepad isn't focused. For trigonometry, logarithms and powers, switch on <a href="/guides/calcsolver-code-mode">CalcSolver Code Mode Guide</a> and type them out in full, like <code>sin(45)</code> or <code>2^10</code>.</p>


        <h3>Using the Scratch Notes Feature</h3>
        <p>Underneath the calculator there's a ruled notepad for working out a problem as you go - jotting down the steps of an equation, a formula you keep forgetting, or an answer you need in a minute. It saves automatically in your browser, so it's still there when you come back, and nothing you write is uploaded anywhere.</p>
        <div class="sc-ad sc-ad-section" id="sc-gam-notepad"></div>
        <p>You can undo and redo, drop the calculator's current answer straight into the page with one tap, copy the lot to your clipboard, download it as a <strong>.txt</strong> file, or print it (choose "Save as PDF" in the print dialog to keep a copy). Handy for showing your working on homework you have to hand in.</p>

        <h3>How to Use Scientific Calculator Online</h3>
        <figure style="margin:.5rem 0 1rem;text-align:center;">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/assets/img/solvecalc-free-calc-solver-online.webp" type="image/webp">
            <img src="/assets/img/solvecalc-free-calc-solver-online.webp" alt="How to use CalcSolver online - step-by-step guide to SolveCalc's free scientific calculator" title="How to use CalcSolver online" width="532" height="469" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px;margin:0 auto;display:block;" loading="lazy">
          </picture>
          <figcaption style="font-size:.85rem;color:var(--text-muted,#888);margin-top:.5rem;">How to use CalcSolver online</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Click numbers and operators to build your expression. Hit <strong>=</strong> to get the answer. <strong>AC</strong> clears everything. <strong>⌫</strong> deletes the last character. For trig and log functions, type the number first then click the function - so type <code>45</code> then hit <strong>sin</strong> to get sin(45°) = 0.7071. That's all there is to it.</p>

        <h3>What It Actually Does</h3>
        <p>I wanted a calculator that didn't require an app download or a login. Just open it and go. SolveCalc handles the things you actually need - basic arithmetic, trig, logs, square roots, powers, percentages, and parentheses for controlling order of operations. The constants π and e are one click away. It works the same on a phone, a Chromebook, or a desktop.</p>
        <p>I've used it to double-check sin values during a physics problem at 11pm when I didn't have my TI-84 nearby. It worked fine. That's the whole point - reliability when you need it, zero friction to get there.</p>

        <div class="sc-ad sc-ad-section" id="sc-gam-themes"></div>
        <h3>The Seven Themes</h3>
        <p>There are seven calculator skins: Classic dark, iOS style, Android minimal, light minimal, pastel candy, teal flat, and scientific device. I added them because different people genuinely have preferences and staring at a calculator for an hour during homework matters. The pastel one is personally my favourite. Switch them using the buttons above the calculator - it's instant, no reload.</p>

        <h3>Who Uses This</h3>
        <p>Mostly students - high school and university. A lot of people use it on Chromebooks at school because they can't install apps. Teachers use it to demonstrate live in class without pulling out a physical calculator. I've also seen it used for quick percentage checks while shopping, salary calculations, and tipping at restaurants. Basically anyone who needs a calculator right now and doesn't want to dig through their phone's app drawer.</p>
        <p>The math tools section below handles the more specialised stuff - fraction arithmetic, unit conversions, equation solving, graphing. If you came here for any of those, scroll down or click directly from the tools grid.</p>


        <h3>What Can This CalcSolver Solve?</h3>
        <p>Here's what the calcsolver handles, and which tool to reach for:</p>
        <table><caption class="visually-hidden">What Can This CalcSolver Solve?</caption>
          <tr><th>Problem type</th><th>Example</th><th>Where to solve it</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Arithmetic</td><td>1284 + 976 - 340</td><td>Main calculator</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Trigonometry</td><td>sin(45), cos(60), tan(30)</td><td>Main calculator</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Logarithms</td><td>log(100), ln(50)</td><td>Main calculator</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Powers and roots</td><td>2^10, √144</td><td>Main calculator or <a href="/tools/exponent-calculator">Exponent Calculator</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fractions</td><td>3/8 + 5/12</td><td><a href="/tools/fraction-calculator">Fraction Calculator</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Percentages</td><td>15% of 240</td><td><a href="/tools/percentage-calculator">Percentage Calculator</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Linear equations</td><td>3x + 5 = 20</td><td><a href="/tools/equation-solver">Equation Solver</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Averages and spread</td><td>mean, median, mode</td><td><a href="/tools/statistics-calculator">Statistics Calculator</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Area and perimeter</td><td>circle, triangle, rectangle</td><td><a href="/tools/geometry-calculator">Geometry Calculator</a></td></tr>
        </table>

        <div class="sc-ad sc-ad-section" id="sc-gam-worked"></div>
        <h3>Worked Examples: Solving Real Problems</h3>
        <p>Here are common problems people bring to a calcsolver, worked through step by step so you can follow the method and check your own answer.</p>

        <h4>How to calculate a percentage of a number</h4>
        <p><strong>Problem:</strong> What is 15% of 240?</p>
        <p>Convert the percentage to a decimal by dividing by 100, then multiply:</p>
        <div class="math-box">15 ÷ 100 = 0.15<br>0.15 × 240 = <strong>36</strong></div>
        <p>So 15% of 240 is 36. The <a href="/tools/percentage-calculator">Percentage Calculator</a> does this in one step, including the reverse case (what percent is 36 of 240).</p>

        <h4>How to calculate a discount</h4>
        <p><strong>Problem:</strong> A $60 jacket is 20% off. What do you pay?</p>
        <div class="math-box">Discount: 60 × 0.20 = 12<br>Final price: 60 − 12 = <strong>$48</strong></div>
        <p>A faster route is to multiply by what you still pay: 100% − 20% = 80%, so 60 × 0.80 = $48 directly.</p>

        <h4>How to add fractions with different denominators</h4>
        <p><strong>Problem:</strong> 3/8 + 5/12</p>
        <p>Find the lowest common denominator. 8 and 12 both divide into 24, so convert both fractions:</p>
        <div class="math-box">3/8 = 9/24 &nbsp;(multiply top and bottom by 3)<br>5/12 = 10/24 &nbsp;(multiply top and bottom by 2)<br>9/24 + 10/24 = <strong>19/24</strong></div>
        <p>19 and 24 share no common factor, so 19/24 is the simplified answer. The <a href="/tools/fraction-calculator">Fraction Calculator</a> shows this working visually.</p>

        <h4>How to solve a linear equation</h4>
        <p><strong>Problem:</strong> Solve 3x + 5 = 20</p>
        <p>Isolate x by undoing each operation on both sides:</p>
        <div class="math-box">3x + 5 = 20<br>3x = 20 − 5 = 15 &nbsp;(subtract 5 from both sides)<br>x = 15 ÷ 3 = <strong>5</strong></div>
        <p>Check it by substituting back: 3(5) + 5 = 20. ✓ The <a href="/tools/equation-solver">Equation Solver</a> shows every step for equations like this.</p>

        <h4>How to use sin, cos and tan</h4>
        <p><strong>Problem:</strong> Find sin(45°)</p>
        <p>Type 45, then press <strong>sin</strong>. Make sure the calculator is in degrees, not radians:</p>
        <div class="math-box">sin(45°) = <strong>0.7071</strong> &nbsp;(exactly √2 ÷ 2)</div>
        <p>This is the single most common trig mistake: sin(45) in radian mode returns 0.8509, a completely different angle. Check your mode before trusting a trig answer.</p>

        <h4>How to calculate powers and roots</h4>
        <p><strong>Problem:</strong> What is 2<sup>10</sup>, and what is √144?</p>
        <div class="math-box">2^10 = <strong>1024</strong><br>√144 = <strong>12</strong> &nbsp;(because 12 × 12 = 144)</div>
        <p>In Code Mode you can type these directly as <code>2^10</code> and <code>sqrt(144)</code> rather than clicking buttons.</p>

        <h4>How to find the mean of a set of numbers</h4>
        <p><strong>Problem:</strong> Find the mean of 12, 15, 18, 20, and 25.</p>
        <div class="math-box">Sum: 12 + 15 + 18 + 20 + 25 = 90<br>Count: 5 values<br>Mean: 90 ÷ 5 = <strong>18</strong></div>
        <p>The <a href="/tools/statistics-calculator">Statistics Calculator</a> also returns the median and mode for the same data set.</p>

        <h4>How to work out a tip</h4>
        <p><strong>Problem:</strong> An 18% tip on a $47.50 bill.</p>
        <div class="math-box">Tip: 47.50 × 0.18 = 8.55<br>Total: 47.50 + 8.55 = <strong>$56.05</strong></div>
        <p>For a rough mental estimate, 10% of the bill is $4.75, half of that is about $2.38, and adding them gives roughly $7.13 for 15%.</p>


        <h3>CalcSolver vs a Phone Calculator App</h3>
        <p>The default calculator app on a phone is fine for adding up a bill, but it hides scientific functions behind a rotate gesture, shows only the current number instead of the whole expression, and can't be opened on a locked-down school Chromebook. A browser-based calcsolver avoids all three problems: nothing to install, the full expression stays visible while you build it, and it opens on any device with a browser.</p>
        <p>That last point matters more than it sounds. Most school devices block app installs entirely, so a web calcsolver is often the only option a student actually has during class.</p>

