              <td>Manual app updates</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Allowed in exams</th>
              <td><span class="cs-na">Check your exam board</span></td>
              <td><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check"></i> Usually the approved option</td>
              <td><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-xmark"></i> Phones normally banned</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="cs-compare-note">A handheld calculator is still the right tool in an exam hall, where phones and browsers are not allowed. For homework, revision and everyday classwork, a free browser-based scientific calculator does more: nothing to install, nothing to charge, the whole expression stays on screen, and the fraction, percentage, graphing and statistics tools sit one click away.</p>

    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- ══ PERSONAL STORY SECTION ══ -->
  <section class="home-section home-story-section">
    <div class="home-story-inner">
      <h2>Why I Made This</h2>
      <p class="story-byline">By <a href="/about#author">Sam Rivera</a>, creator of SolveCalc</p>
      <img class="home-story-img" src="/assets/img/calcsolver-why-i-made-this.webp" alt="Working on a laptop while building SolveCalc" loading="lazy" width="240" height="160">
      <p>Honest reason - I was frustrated. Every time I needed a quick scientific calculator online, I either landed on a site that looked like it hadn't been updated since 2009, or something that demanded an extension, an account, or a full-screen ad. I just wanted to punch in sin(45) and move on with my life.</p>
      <p>So I built <a href="/about">SolveCalc</a>. The goal was simple: load fast, work on any device, no friction.</p>
      <p>I specifically wanted it to feel decent on a school Chromebook, because that's where a lot of students are stuck. No app stores, no admin permissions. Just a URL and a browser.</p>
      <p>The six themes came later, after I noticed I was using the calculator for long stretches during homework sessions. Looking at a harsh black-on-white or glaring white-on-black layout for an hour gets tiring.</p>
      <p>The pastel theme was the first one I actually liked using for extended periods - soft enough that it doesn't strain your eyes, but still clearly readable. I left all six in because people genuinely have preferences.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/tools">math tools</a> section grew out of the same idea. Fraction arithmetic, GCF/LCM, triangle solving - these are things students look up constantly, usually ending up on a mediocre tool surrounded by clutter. I tried to make each one actually explain what it's doing, not just spit out a number.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ CLIENT-SIDE CALCULATION ══ -->
  <section class="home-section">
    <h2 class="home-section-title">Every Calculation Runs in Your Browser, Not on a Server</h2>
    <div class="home-story-inner">
      <img class="home-story-img" src="/assets/img/calcsolver-browser-privacy.webp" alt="Close-up of a computer keyboard" loading="lazy" width="240" height="128">
      <p>Most people never think about where a calc solver's math actually happens. On a lot of calculator sites, typing an expression sends it off to a server somewhere, waits for a response, and prints whatever comes back.</p>
      <p>You don't notice this until the server is slow, or down, or your school's WiFi throttles the request - and suddenly a simple square root takes three seconds.</p>
      <p>SolveCalc (or solve calc, as plenty of people type it) doesn't work that way. Every calculation - basic arithmetic, trig, logarithms, fraction simplification, the <a href="/tools/graphing-calculator">graphing calculator</a> plotting your equation - runs entirely in JavaScript, inside your own browser, on your own device.</p>
      <p>Nothing you type gets sent anywhere. There's no round trip, no queue, no "server busy" message. The answer appears the instant you press Enter because there was never anywhere else for it to go.</p>
      <p>This matters for two practical reasons beyond speed. First, privacy: whatever you're calculating - a grade, a budget, a homework answer - never leaves your device, isn't logged, and isn't tied to an account, because there's no account and no backend collecting it.</p>
      <p>Second, reliability: once the page has loaded, the calculator keeps working even on a flaky connection, since it isn't waiting on anything else to respond. That's also why it holds up fine on older school Chromebooks and slower networks where server-dependent tools visibly lag.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ WHY THIS IS AN EFFICIENT CALCSOLVER ══ -->
  <section class="home-section">
    <h2 class="home-section-title">Built to Be an Efficient CalcSolver, Not Just a Calculator</h2>
    <div class="home-story-inner">
      <img class="home-story-img" src="/assets/img/calcsolver-scientific-calculator.webp" alt="A scientific calculator" loading="lazy" width="240" height="240">
      <p>"Efficient" gets thrown around a lot in software descriptions without meaning much, so here's what it actually means for SolveCalc as a calc solver, concretely.</p>
      <p>The page itself is lightweight - no bloated framework, no dozens of tracking scripts fighting for the main thread - so it loads fast even on a throttled connection. There's no sign-up wall standing between you and the calculator, no email capture, no "continue as guest" button to hunt for. You land on the page and the calculator is already there.</p>
      <p>Efficiency also shows up in how the tool is organized. Instead of one calculator trying to do everything badly, SolveCalc splits into the main scientific calculator plus <a href="/tools">20+ dedicated tools</a> - fractions, percentages, geometry, statistics - each built around the one problem type it solves, with the working shown visually instead of buried.</p>
      <p>And for anyone doing a lot of calculations back to back, <a href="/guides/calcsolver-code-mode">CalcSolver Code Mode Guide</a> means never touching the mouse: type <code>sqrt(144)</code>, press Enter, done, on to the next line.</p>
      <p>None of that is an accident. A calcsolver that takes ten seconds to load, buries the input field under three ad units, or forces an account just to see a fraction simplified isn't actually saving anyone time - it just moved the friction somewhere less obvious.</p>
      <p>SolveCalc is built around the opposite bet: the fastest, least annoying path from "I have a math problem" to "I have the answer" wins, every time, regardless of what else is on the page. That is what a solve calc tool should be - open it, type, done.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ CALCSOLVER CODE MODE ══ -->
  <section class="home-section">
    <h2 class="home-section-title">CalcSolver Code Mode</h2>
    <div class="home-story-inner">
      <img class="home-story-img" src="/assets/img/blog/calcsolver-code-mode.webp" alt="Typing a math expression in CalcSolver Code Mode" loading="lazy" width="240" height="135">
      <p>There's a faster way to use the calculator that most people walk right past. CalcSolver code mode lets you type expressions directly using function codes - so instead of hunting for the sin button and clicking it, you just type <code>sin(45)</code> and press Enter.</p>
      <p>Same result, half the clicks. Once the muscle memory kicks in, you'll rarely touch the buttons.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/guides/calcsolver-code-mode">full code list</a> covers everything on the button panel and a bit more: <code>sqrt()</code>, <code>log()</code>, <code>ln()</code>, <code>asin()</code>, <code>e^x</code>, powers with <code>^</code>, factorials with <code>!</code>, and percentage with <code>%</code>.</p>
      <p>You can chain them into longer expressions - something like <code>sqrt(b^2 - 4*a*c)</code> - and see the whole thing before you hit Enter, which cuts down on errors from clicking in the wrong order.</p>
      <p>It works best on a laptop or desktop where your hands are already on the keyboard. No setting to turn on - just click the calculator and start typing. The Code Mode switch above the display is separate: it's for entering 4-digit codes. The <a href="/guides/calcsolver-code-mode">complete CalcSolver code guide</a> has the full table of codes, keyboard shortcuts, and a few combinations most users never discover on their own.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Consolidated: this used to be 3 separate 300x250 breaks
       (section2/2b/3) across "Every Calculation Runs...", "Why This Is an
       Efficient CalcSolver", and "Code Mode" - by this depth on an
       18-section homepage, realistic scroll-through is low enough that 3
       back-to-back slots were mostly cannibalizing each other rather than
       adding real incremental viewable impressions. One slot here instead. -->
  <div class="sc-ad sc-ad-section" id="sc-gam-section3"></div>

