  <!-- ══ FAQ ══ -->
  <section class="home-section home-faq-section" id="faq">
    <h2 class="home-section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="home-faq-list">

      <details class="home-faq-item" open>
        <summary class="home-faq-q">What is SolveCalc?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">SolveCalc is a free web platform of math tools and calculators built for school students, available only at solvecalc.net. There's no account to create, nothing to download, and nothing costs money - every calculation runs in your own browser. Alongside a full scientific calculator, SolveCalc includes 34+ dedicated tools for fractions, percentages, geometry, statistics, graphing, and family finance, plus reference guides covering algebra, number theory, and more.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="home-faq-item">

        <summary class="home-faq-q">Is it SolveCalc or Solve Calc?</summary>

        <div class="home-faq-a">Both. "Solve Calc" with a space and "SolveCalc" as one word point to the same site, solvecalc.net. People type it either way, and search engines treat the two as the same brand - whichever one brought you here, this is the free calc solver you were looking for, with the scientific calculator, 34+ math tools and Code Mode all in one place.</div>

      </details>

      <details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">What is a calcsolver?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">A <a href="/guides/what-is-calcsolver">calcsolver</a> (also written calc solver) is a free online calculator that solves math problems instantly in your browser - trigonometry, logarithms, square roots, fractions, percentages, and algebra. SolveCalc is a full calcsolver: it works through the whole expression as you build it, so you can see exactly what you've typed before hitting equals. No download, no account, works on any device.</div>
      </details>

            <details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">What can the SolveCalc CalcSolver solve?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">Arithmetic, algebra, trigonometry, logarithms, powers, roots, fractions and percentages. With Code Mode on you can type expressions straight from the keyboard, such as sin(45), sqrt(16), log(100), 2^10 or 5!, and press Enter for the answer. For problems that need more than one line, SolveCalc also has 34+ dedicated tools, including an <a href="/tools/equation-solver">equation solver</a>, a <a href="/tools/fraction-calculator">fraction calculator</a> and a <a href="/tools/statistics-calculator">statistics calculator</a>.</div>
      </details>

<details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">How is a CalcSolver different from a regular calculator?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">A regular calculator makes you press one button at a time. A CalcSolver like SolveCalc lets you type the whole expression, including functions like sin, log and square roots, and solves it at once, so it is faster for homework and easier to check. It runs in your browser with nothing to install, and works on school Chromebooks, phones and laptops.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">Is there a free calcsolver online?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">Yes. SolveCalc is a completely free calcsolver - no sign-up, no subscription, and no app to install. Alongside the main scientific calculator there are 20+ dedicated tools for fractions, percentages, equations, geometry, statistics, and graphing.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">Is SolveCalc free? Do I need an account?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">Completely free, no account required. You open the page and start calculating. There's nothing to sign up for, no paywall, and no downloads. Your theme preference is saved locally in your browser - nothing is stored on any server.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">What math functions are supported?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">The calculator handles sin, cos, tan (degrees), log, ln, square roots, powers, percentages, π and e, and full bracket support. The math tools section adds graphing, fraction arithmetic, GCF/LCM, statistics, equation solving, triangle solving, unit conversions, and more.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">Does SolveCalc work on mobile and school Chromebooks?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">Yes. It's just a web page - no installs, no plugins. The layout adapts to smaller screens and buttons are tap-friendly. School Chromebooks and filtered networks work fine since nothing needs to be installed.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">Is SolveCalc safe for schools? Is it FERPA and COPPA friendly?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">Yes. Every calculation runs entirely in the student's own browser - nothing typed into SolveCalc is ever sent to a server, logged, or stored anywhere. There's no account, no sign-up, and no student data collected in the first place, which is exactly what FERPA and COPPA compliance requires of classroom tools. It's also why SolveCalc works on locked-down school networks and Chromebooks with nothing to install or approve.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">What are CalcSolver codes and what is CalcSolver code mode?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">CalcSolver code mode is the keyboard-driven way to use the calculator. Instead of clicking buttons, you type function codes directly - like sin(45), sqrt(16), log(100), or 2^10 - and press Enter for the result. The full list of calcsolver codes covers every function on the button panel plus combinations like factorial (5!), percentage (15%), and any-base logarithms. See the <a href="/guides/calcsolver-code-mode">CalcSolver code mode guide</a> for the complete list and examples.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="home-faq-item">
        <summary class="home-faq-q">Is SolveCalc the same as SolveCalc.pro, SolveCalc.online, or CalcSolver.pro?</summary>
        <div class="home-faq-a">No. SolveCalc is only available at solvecalc.net. Similarly named sites on other domain endings are not affiliated with this project - if you're not sure you're on the right one, check the address bar.</div>
      </details>

    </div>

  </section>


  
